            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>First Step After Account Suspension</h2>
<p>Upon receiving a Google Ads account suspension notification, don't rush into preparing materials. The first step is to log into the account dashboard and locate the specific violation reason and policy terms. Google marks the violation type in the email, but more detailed information is available on the "Policies and Terms" page, including the corresponding violation codes.</p>
<p>Common suspension reasons fall into three main areas:<br />
- Shopping or service policy violations (e.g., false advertising, prohibited products)<br />
- Circumvention behaviors (e.g., manipulating ad bidding, domain redirects)<br />
- Content policy violations (e.g., hate speech, misleading content)</p>
<p>Identifying the violation type is essential before addressing it. Otherwise, no matter how detailed your appeal materials are, you're wasting time.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What's Most Critical in Appeal Materials</h2>
<p>A common mistake when preparing appeal materials: spending excessive space explaining "we'll be more careful in the future." In reality, Google's review team wants to see <strong>specific corrective actions</strong>, not promises.</p>
<p>Effective appeal materials typically include three elements:<br />
1. Clearly acknowledge the violation without making excuses<br />
2. Describe specific corrective measures already taken (e.g., removing violating creatives, modifying landing pages)<br />
3. Provide verifiable evidence (screenshots, new links, new policy pages)</p>
<p>When assisting clients with appeal materials, we recommend first organizing violating ad groups and creatives into a table, mapping each violation point to its remediation progress. Reviewers process applications faster when they see clear correspondence.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How to Find Violation Codes</h2>
<p>If the violation code isn't directly shown in the email, follow this path:</p>
<p><strong>Help Center → Policies and Terms → Account-level Restrictions → View Specific Violations</strong></p>
<p>Each violation type has a unique code, such as "TCS1001" indicating a specific shopping policy violation. Record this code and reference it when writing your appeal email to help reviewers locate the issue faster.</p>
<p>In some cases, the violation code may not accurately reflect the actual violation. In such situations, don't directly dispute the code. Instead, describe your understanding of the violating scenario and ask reviewers to confirm the specific issue.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How Long Does It Take to Get a Response</h2>
<p>Google's official reference time is 1-3 business days, but actual processing periods vary significantly. Based on client cases we've handled, simple creative violations typically get responses in 3-5 business days, while complex policy-level appeals may require 2-3 weeks.</p>
<p>If there's no response after 10 business days, you can send a query through official support channels (the "Help and Feedback" section in the ads dashboard), stating when you submitted the appeal and providing the reference number. Excessive follow-ups may actually affect processing priority.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How to Improve Success Rate on Resubmission</h2>
<p>After a first appeal rejection, many people make two mistakes: either submitting the exact same materials unchanged or filing emotional complaints. In reality, resubmission requires new evidence or more specific remediation explanations.</p>
<p>Effective resubmission strategies:<br />
- Add evidence materials not provided in the first submission<br />
- Address each rejection reason individually, explaining why the first submission was insufficient to prove remediation<br />
- If there's a policy interpretation disagreement, cite Google's official policy text, explaining how your business scenario differs from the violation description</p>
<p>We handled a case where a utility app was suspended for "tricking users into clicking." The first appeal submitted screenshots of modified creatives but didn't provide a comparison between old and new materials. On resubmission, we added a clear change对照表 and user behavior data proving the modified creatives no longer triggered deceptive interaction mechanisms. The appeal succeeded.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Common Reasons for Appeal Rejection</h2>
<p>Appeals are typically rejected not for insufficient detail but for wrong direction. Here are three common rejection reasons:</p>
<p><strong>1. Root Cause Not Addressed</strong><br />
Only the flagged ads were deleted without checking whether similar creatives violate the same policies. Reviewers will continue rejecting the appeal.</p>
<p><strong>2. Remediation Measures Not Verifiable</strong><br />
Claiming "remediated" without providing accessible links or screenshots. Reviewers cannot confirm whether remediation actually took place.</p>
<p><strong>3. Multiple Violations in Same Account</strong><br />
Repeated violations of the same type in a short period. Even if the current appeal succeeds, the account enters high-risk monitoring, restricting future advertising.</p>
<p>The core of account appeals is making reviewers confident that the problem has been completely resolved and mechanisms are in place to prevent recurrence. When preparing materials, focus on "verifiability," and your appeal success rate will significantly improve.</p>
